#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120684} :activity/id #uuid "72f4cece-c3b1-5eb9-88d1-e1d563f3b9f1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048181 :topic/id #uuid "865bb9f9-35c6-5dde-8b22-02c7952508da" :topic/math-academy-id 1854} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121039 :tutorial/content "The **null space** of a matrix $A$ is the set of all the solutions of the equation $A\\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{0},$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Null}(A).$ \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/c3/c3bf583e98daad3fa753880b96c6ba85bce697f3434690977c5679b64ffd4882.svg)\n\nThe null space consists of all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ For example, the vectors ![](images/9d/9d94e47625ee3c9d6fdff788db07e925ec2375e6c57cc6c1d8897ba423749abf.svg) ![](images/e5/e5a5f6b93eccdf25da72ea39af64a4a5cca7b0a03d5f469cb85554dc6dc12f88.svg) and $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ all satisfy the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ so they are all elements of the null space:\n\n![](images/5e/5ededa27ee77302b18683f91e37d854a68cd3ba6ceacc7e2a6068a2730b4a9bf.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "3d284a1f-e3a9-5b48-97e4-d621a81b5237" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3204 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4c3bb184-f74c-5977-a8e6-98bdcb8a2dd8" :step/math-academy-id 17496 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119812 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119623 :question/id #uuid "fe2f3934-fa5c-5a59-be7e-00b6e4889063" :question/math-academy-id "e-4810"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3c3466c-dfb0-5b5f-b556-ce57a1d78519" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417739}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Vector Lies in the Null Space of a Given Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8a689cc9-53dd-54fa-a611-9d759b6620a8" :step/math-academy-id 17498 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120686}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119624 :question/id #uuid "e38db5fa-1753-5176-bb5e-a077ea63ddec" :question/math-academy-id "e-4812"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9171c6fb-ad0b-538f-b982-a2502a20e253" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049151}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417741}] :knowledge-point/title "Solve a Variable in a Vector Lying in the Null Space of a Given Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "99922077-a3d5-5752-963a-c25d4e87ccf4" :step/math-academy-id 17500 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120751}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119625 :question/id #uuid "1e59cd29-ea54-5b90-8215-60c81d1b6dbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4814"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "465ce2b7-352e-5050-a5e4-0753fbacebf5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417742}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Null Space of a Given Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "5062db3d-b1a3-544d-a0cd-7dd7a74b40af" :step/math-academy-id 17502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120751 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121063 :tutorial/content "Let's again consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/8f/8fa69630c71d5106ed04a5281681992687174891b1c0978521c12e914cb3a0b0.svg)\n\nTo find an expression for the null space using set-builder notation, we need to find all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Let's start by considering the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\, \\mathbf{0}]$ and finding its reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,0] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st column). The second column is not a pivot column.\n\nNow, we convert the reduced matrix back into a system, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $\\color{blue}x_1$ is a basic variable (it corresponds to the pivot column), while $\\color{red}x_2$ is a free variable (it corresponds to the non-pivot column). Because there is a free variable, the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions. \n\nFrom the first equation we get ${\\color{blue}x_1}=-3{\\color{red}x_2},$ so the set of all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is\n![](images/8c/8c5541adb07cb58136d71fb4e3218981f1717b929f22acbe55d133cd0c395870.svg)\n\n**Note:** It is not necessary to consider the full augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{0}]$ since the last column won't be changed during the process of reduction. Really, we just need to find the reduced row echelon form of the matrix $A$ and then set up the corresponding system." :tutorial/id #uuid "895e6e15-cec9-50e1-9bed-dff731650d72" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3943 :tutorial/title "Writing the Null Space using Set-Builder Notation"} :step/id #uuid "7af33686-5798-591e-a725-45ae0719e381" :step/math-academy-id 23359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120687}}] :activity/title "The Null Space of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120048}]}
